WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.856 --> 00:00:18.982
The last time I'd been at a horse sale, I'd stood out front with the strong, useful horses.

2
00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:24.905
This time I found myself in the back among the old, broken-down, used-up creatures.

3
00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:28.846
The people who came to look at us weren't much better.

4
00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:32.568
They were mostly poor and hopeless-looking themselves.

5
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.361
Sometimes a shopper from the nicer part of the sale would wander past.

6
00:00:38.742 --> 00:00:41.723
One of these was a gentleman farmer with a kind, ruddy face.

7
00:00:42.423 --> 00:00:43.244
A boy was with him.

8
00:00:44.384 --> 00:00:45.885
The gentleman saw me and stopped.

9
00:00:47.045 --> 00:00:48.526
I pricked my ears and looked at him.

10
00:00:48.546 --> 00:00:52.067
"Look, Willie," the man said to the boy.

11
00:00:52.087 --> 00:00:55.309
"Now there's a horse that's known better days."

12
00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:59.710
"Do you think he was ever a carriage horse, Grandpa?"

13
00:01:00.071 --> 00:01:00.831
the boy asked.

14
00:01:00.851 --> 00:01:04.997
"I wouldn't be surprised," his grandfather replied.

15
00:01:05.017 --> 00:01:08.499
"He might have been anything when he was young.

16
00:01:09.579 --> 00:01:14.122
If you look beyond his poor condition, you can still see his fine breeding."

17
00:01:15.303 --> 00:01:16.663
He gave me a pat on the neck.

18
00:01:17.424 --> 00:01:21.406
I stretched my nose toward him in response, and the boy stroked my face.

19
00:01:21.426 --> 00:01:25.869
"Look how sweet he is!"

20
00:01:26.249 --> 00:01:26.869
the boy said.

21
00:01:26.889 --> 00:01:32.772
"Can't you buy him and make him young again, like you did with Ladybird?"

22
00:01:32.852 --> 00:01:36.454
"I can't make all old horses young, Willie," the farmer said.

23
00:01:36.474 --> 00:01:40.035
"Besides, Ladybird wasn't that old.

24
00:01:41.135 --> 00:01:43.516
She was just run-down and treated badly."

25
00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:47.418
"How do you know this one's any older, Grandpa?"

26
00:01:47.778 --> 00:01:48.579
Willie replied.

27
00:01:48.599 --> 00:01:55.541
"He still has a nice-looking mane and tail, and his eyes aren't sunken in like some old horses.

28
00:01:56.742 --> 00:01:59.303
At least check his teeth to see his age."

29
00:02:00.503 --> 00:02:01.364
The farmer laughed.

30
00:02:02.508 --> 00:02:05.871
"You're as horsey as your old grandfather," he teased.

31
00:02:07.252 --> 00:02:09.894
The man from Skinner's stable had been listening to all this.

32
00:02:09.914 --> 00:02:13.137
"Your grandson is right, sir," he spoke up.

33
00:02:13.157 --> 00:02:18.121
"This horse isn't terribly old, just tired out from cab work.

34
00:02:19.563 --> 00:02:23.126
I heard the vet say he'd be good as new with six months' rest.

35
00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:27.069
And you won't find a better-tempered animal anywhere."

36
00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:32.305
The farmer talked with him a little more, then felt my legs and looked into my mouth.

37
00:02:34.066 --> 00:02:36.587
"Thirteen or fourteen, I'd guess," he said.

38
00:02:36.607 --> 00:02:38.669
"Could you trot him for us?"

39
00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.290
The other man jogged me in a circle.

40
00:02:42.931 --> 00:02:46.593
I arched my thin neck and stretched out my stiff legs as best I could.

41
00:02:46.613 --> 00:02:50.015
"What's the lowest you'll take for him?"

42
00:02:50.355 --> 00:02:51.215
the farmer asked.

43
00:02:51.235 --> 00:02:53.557
"Five pounds, sir.

44
00:02:54.397 --> 00:02:56.518
That was the lowest price my boss set."

45
00:02:56.538 --> 00:03:01.833
"It's taking a chance," the farmer said with a sigh.

46
00:03:02.954 --> 00:03:05.136
He pulled out some money and handed it to the man.

47
00:03:07.018 --> 00:03:10.120
Young Willie could hardly contain his joy as they led me away.

48
00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:17.206
When they got me home, the farmer told his workers to turn me out in a grassy field

49
00:03:17.266 --> 00:03:17.727
to rest.

50
00:03:20.949 --> 00:03:23.572
He also wanted me to have hay and grain every day.

51
00:03:23.592 --> 00:03:28.476
"He'll be your special project, my boy," he told Willie.

52
00:03:29.464 --> 00:03:31.745
"You're in charge of his care from now on."

53
00:03:33.285 --> 00:03:36.366
Willie was proud of his new job and took it very seriously.

54
00:03:38.847 --> 00:03:43.128
He came to see me every day, spending time with me and making sure I had enough to

55
00:03:43.208 --> 00:03:47.729
eat. His name for me was Old Crony.

56
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:56.032
I spent that winter with complete rest, plenty of food, kind people, and pleasant surroundings.

57
00:03:58.656 --> 00:04:00.878
By spring I was feeling almost young again!

58
00:04:02.299 --> 00:04:05.241
Finally the farmer decided it was time to try me in harness.

59
00:04:07.523 --> 00:04:10.786
He and Willie hooked me to a small carriage and drove me a few miles.

60
00:04:12.487 --> 00:04:15.550
By then my legs were no longer stiff, and I enjoyed the work.

61
00:04:17.811 --> 00:04:20.654
"He's growing young, Willie," the farmer said with a smile.

62
00:04:20.674 --> 00:04:24.937
"We'll give him a little easy work to get him fit again.

63
00:04:26.158 --> 00:04:29.184
By summer he'll be as good as Ladybird.

64
00:04:29.204 --> 00:04:31.565
He's well-trained and has very good gaits."

65
00:04:32.945 --> 00:04:35.786
"I'm so glad you bought him, Grandpa!"

66
00:04:36.086 --> 00:04:36.726
the boy said.

67
00:04:36.746 --> 00:04:40.407
"So am I," the farmer replied.

68
00:04:40.427 --> 00:04:43.328
"But he has you to thank for it, my boy.

69
00:04:44.549 --> 00:04:49.470
Now all we need is to find him a good, safe home where he'll be lightly worked and

70
00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:50.450
highly valued."

71
00:04:51.651 --> 00:04:55.272
For a couple of months, the pair took me out with the carriage fairly often.

72
00:04:57.084 --> 00:05:00.466
Soon I was back in shape and pulling them happily all around the neighborhood.

73
00:05:01.847 --> 00:05:04.448
One day the grooms spent extra time getting me ready.

74
00:05:05.649 --> 00:05:11.092
They made my black coat shine, trimmed the stray hairs off my legs, and polished my hooves.

75
00:05:12.513 --> 00:05:14.214
Even the harness seemed extra clean.

76
00:05:16.155 --> 00:05:19.797
When Willie arrived, he seemed happy and anxious at the same time.

77
00:05:19.817 --> 00:05:23.499
"Do you think they'll like him, Grandpa?"

78
00:05:23.839 --> 00:05:24.720
he asked the farmer.

79
00:05:25.944 --> 00:05:26.985
"I don't know, my boy.

80
00:05:27.005 --> 00:05:30.249
All we can do is give it a try."

81
00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:33.713
They got into the carriage and picked up the reins.

82
00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:36.616
We headed out of the farm and down the road.

83
00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:43.463
I wasn't sure where we were going, but I knew that something unusual was about to happen.
